'''adherence''' (ad-hēr′ens), ''n.'' [< F. ''adhérence'', < ML. ''adhærentia'', < L. ''adhærens'': see ''adherent''.] '''1.''' The act or state of sticking or adhering: rare in a physical sense, ''adhesion'' being commonly used.—'''2.''' Figuratively, the character of being fixed in attachment; fidelity; steady attachment: as, an ''adherence'' to a party or opinions; the act of holding to closely: as, a rigid ''adherence'' to rules.
{{smaller block|A tenacious ''adherence'' to the rights and liberties transmitted from a wise and virtuous ancestry.{{right|''Addison.''}}}}
'''3.''' In ''Scots law'', the return of a husband or wife who has for a time deserted his or her spouse. {{smaller|The spouse who has been deserted may bring an ''action of adherence'' to compel the deserting spouse to return.}}
'''4.''' In ''painting'', the effect of those parts of a picture which, wanting relief, are not detached, and hence appear adhering to the canvas or surface. ''Fairholt.''—'''5.''' In ''logic'' and ''metaph.'', the

