state of being adherent. See ''adherent'', ''a.'', 3.

{{smaller block|=Syn. ''Adherence'', ''Adhesion''. These words are undergoing desynonymization, the moral and figurative sense being limited to ''adherence'', and the physical to ''adhesion'': as, ''adherence'' to the doctrines of Adam Smith; the ''adhesion'' of putty to glass. [Note: ''Adherent'', ''n.'', is not used of physical attachment, nor ''adherent'', ''a.'', of moral attachment. ''Adhere'', ''v.'', is used of either.]}}
{{smaller block|If he departs in any degree from strict ''adherence'' to these rules, . . . he not only departs from rule, but commits an act of treachery and baseness.
{{right|''Gladstone'', Kin beyond Sea, p. 210.}}}}
{{smaller block|Writing and drawing with chalks and pencils depend on the ''adhesion'' of solids.
{{right|''Atkinson'', tr. of Ganot’s Physics, p. 87.}}}}

'''adherency†''' (ạd-hēr′ẹn-si), ''n.'' [As ''adherence'': see ''-ency''.] '''1.''' The state of being adherent.
{{smaller block|''Adherencies'' and admirations of men’s persons.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'' (?), Artif. Handsomeness, p. 172.}}}}
'''2.''' That which is adherent.
{{smaller block|Vices have a native ''adherency'' of vexation.
{{right|''Decay of Christ. Piety.''}}}}

'''adherent''' (ad-hēr′ẹnt), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< F. ''adhérent'', < L. ''adhæren(t-)s'', ppr. of ''adhærere'': see ''adhere''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Sticking; clinging; adhering.
{{smaller block|<poem>Close to the cliff with both his hands he clung,
And stuck ''adherent'', and suspended hung.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Odyssey, l. 547.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''bot.'', congenitally united, as parts that are normally separate: generally used as equivalent to ''adnate''. See cut under ''adnate''.—'''3.''' In ''logic'' and ''metaph.'', accidentally connected; not belonging to the nature of a thing; not inherent: as, if a cloth is wet, its wetness is a

